re billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.4514998529555926},"bill":{"id":"119-hjres-54","congress":119,"billType":"hjres","billNumber":"54","title":"Proposing an amendment to the Constitution of the United States providing that the rights protected and extended by the Constitution are the rights of natural persons only.","introducedDate":"2025-02-12","latestActionDate":"2025-02-12","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on the Judiciary.","policyArea":"Civil Rights and Liberties, Minority Issues","subjects":["Constitution and constitutional amendments","Elections, voting, political campaign regulation","First Amendment rights"],"sponsorBioguideId":"J000298","cosponsorCount":77,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/54","industries":[{"industry":"finance-banking","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The amendment would subject banks and financial firms to new constitutional limits on political spending and strip them of constitutional rights currently recognized for corporations."},{"industry":"tech","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"Technology companies would lose constitutional protections as corporate entities and face new restrictions on election-related expenditures and contributions."},{"industry":"energy-fossil","confidence":0.8,"rationale":"Oil, gas, and coal companies would be subject to campaign finance limits and lose constitutional rights currently available to corporate entities."},{"industry":"pharma","confidence":0.8,"rationale":"Pharmaceutical and device manufacturers would face new restrictions on political spending and lose constitutional protections as artificial entities."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"Law firms and lobbying organizations would operate under new campaign finance regulations and potentially altered constitutional frameworks for corporate clients."},{"industry":"media-entertainment","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Media corporations would lose constitutional rights as artificial entities, potentially affecting their ability to make election-related expenditures and editorial decisions."}],"plainSummary":"This proposed constitutional amendment would establish that only individual human beings—not corporations or other artificial entities—hold rights under the Constitution. It would require all levels of government to regulate or limit campaign contributions and spending, including candidates' own funds, to ensure equal political access regardless of wealth. The amendment would also mandate public disclosure of all permitted contributions and prevent courts from treating campaign spending as protected speech under the First Amendment.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who votes.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"W000788","score":0.04129443420932792},"overlapCount":75},"meaning":{"percentile":80.8487143082505,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.4514998529555926}},{"billId":"119-hjres-121","bioguideId":"P000034","cycle":2026,"score":0.015890684951767715,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":64350,"donorShare":0.03154102538966768,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.00940697248463773},{"industry":"media-entertainment","donorAmount":39000,"donorShare":0.019115772963434956,"billConfidence":0.65,"contribution":0.004359737693414991},{"industry":"finance-banking","donorAmount":13000,"donorShare":0.006371924321144986,"billConfidence":0.55,"contribution":0.0012296696058349975},{"industry":"ideological-single-issue","donorAmount":6500,"donorShare":0.003185962160572493,"billConfidence":0.8,"contribution":0.0008943051678799982}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.4514998529555926},"bill":{"id":"119-hjres-121","congress":119,"billType":"hjres","billNumber":"121","title":"Proposing an amendment to the Constitution of the United States relating to contributions and expenditures intended to affect elections.","introducedDate":"2025-09-11","latestActionDate":"2025-09-11","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on the Judiciary.","policyArea":"Government Operations and Politics","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"S001205","cosponsorCount":46,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/121","industries":[{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"Campaign finance regulation directly affects lobbying firms and election-law practitioners who advise on contribution and expenditure compliance."},{"industry":"ideological-single-issue","confidence":0.8,"rationale":"Advocacy organizations that spend money on election-related communications would face new regulatory constraints on their expenditures."},{"industry":"media-entertainment","confidence":0.65,"rationale":"Limits on campaign spending could affect demand for political advertising across broadcast, digital, and print media platforms."},{"industry":"finance-banking","confidence":0.55,"rationale":"Financial institutions that make political expenditures or operate PACs could face new restrictions on corporate election spending."}],"plainSummary":"This joint resolution proposes a constitutional amendment that would allow Congress and state governments to set limits on how much money candidates and others can raise and spend to influence elections. It would also permit lawmakers to treat corporations and other legal entities differently from individual people, including by banning corporate spending on elections. The amendment would require ratification by the states to take effect.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who deals with a federal agency.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"F000454","score":0.04685049700852134},"overlapCount":46},"meaning":{"percentile":65.51506546773939,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.4514998529555926}},{"billId":"119-hr-9943","bioguideId":"P000034","cycle":2026,"score":0.01205764140770513,"matches":[{"industry":"hospitality","donorAmount":32950,"donorShare":0.01615037741397902,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.005700133204933773},{"industry":"retail-consumer","donorAmount":25500,"donorShare":0.012498774629938241,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.004411332222331144},{"industry":"construction","donorAmount":13500,"donorShare":0.006616998333496716,"billConfidence":0.5,"contribution":0.0019461759804402106}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.4514998529555926},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-9943","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"9943","title":"To amend the Small Business Act to help owners and employees of small business concerns understand their rights and responsibilities with respect to immigration enforcement, and for other purposes.","introducedDate":"2026-07-23","latestActionDate":"2026-07-23","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on Small Business.","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"V000081","cosponsorCount":15,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/9943","industries":[{"industry":"retail-consumer","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"Small businesses in retail and food service frequently employ immigrant workers and would be directly affected by new immigration-enforcement guidance requirements."},{"industry":"hospitality","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"Hotels, restaurants, and tourism businesses are major small-business employers in sectors with significant immigrant workforces and would face new compliance obligations."},{"industry":"construction","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Construction firms, many of which are small businesses, employ immigrant workers and would be subject to new requirements regarding immigration-enforcement interactions."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would amend the Small Business Act to require assistance helping small business owners and their employees understand their rights and responsibilities when dealing with immigration enforcement. The source material does not provide detail on what specific programs, resources, or obligations would be created or changed.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"We cannot tell from the labels on this bill who it reaches. The subject is too broad to say.","confidence":"title-only"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"V000081","score":0.022168899423477717},"overlapCount":14},"meaning":{"percentile":54.42498816848084,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.4514998529555926}},{"billId":"119-hr-51","bioguideId":"P000034","cycle":2026,"score":0.012049843598221206,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":64350,"donorShare":0.03154102538966768,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.00860209783354573},{"industry":"real-estate","donorAmount":20500,"donorShare":0.010048034506420939,"billConfidence":0.7,"contribution":0.003197101888406662},{"industry":"government","donorAmount":1250,"donorShare":0.0006126850308793255,"billConfidence":0.9,"contribution":0.000250643876268815}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.4514998529555926},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-51","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"51","title":"Washington, D.C. Admission Act","introducedDate":"2025-01-03","latestActionDate":"2025-01-03","latestActionText":"Referred to the Committee on Oversight and Government Reform, and in addition to the Committees on Rules, Armed Services, the Judiciary, and Energy and Commerce, for a period to be subsequently determined by the Speaker, in each case for consideration of such provisions as fall within the jurisdiction of the committee concerned.","policyArea":"Government Operations and Politics","subjects":["Congressional districts and representation","Congressional elections","Constitution and constitutional amendments","District of Columbia","Elections, voting, political campaign regulation","Government buildings, facilities, and property","House of Representatives","Legislative rules and procedure","Members of Congress","Presidents and presidential powers, Vice Presidents","State and local government operations"],"sponsorBioguideId":"N000147","cosponsorCount":204,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/51","industries":[{"industry":"government","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"The bill fundamentally restructures the governance of the District, creating a new state government and redefining the federal district."},{"industry":"real-estate","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Statehood would change property tax authority and land-use regulation for most of the current District, affecting developers and property owners."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"The transition of DC courts, judicial proceedings, and contractual obligations to state authority would affect legal practice and litigation in the new jurisdiction."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would admit most of the current District of Columbia as the 51st state, called Washington, Douglass Commonwealth, with two senators and one representative in Congress. A small federal district containing the Capitol, White House, Supreme Court, and nearby monuments would remain as the seat of the federal government. The bill sets up a transition process for DC laws, courts, contracts, and obligations to continue under state authority, and creates a commission to manage the shift to statehood.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who votes.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"M001245","score":0.050448841212731466},"overlapCount":197},"meaning":{"percentile":54.36188673292317,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.4514998529555926}},{"billId":"119-hr-1106","bioguideId":"P000034","cycle":2026,"score":0.009913243799627488,"matches":[{"industry":"pharma","donorAmount":69500,"donorShare":0.0340652877168905,"billConfidence":0.75,"contribution":0.008516321929222625},{"industry":"education","donorAmount":13000,"donorShare":0.006371924321144986,"billConfidence":0.5,"contribution":0.0010619873868574976},{"industry":"energy-renewable","donorAmount":2500,"donorShare":0.001225370061758651,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.00024507401235173017},{"industry":"agriculture","donorAmount":1000,"donorShare":0.0004901480247034605,"billConfidence":0.55,"contribution":0.00008986047119563443}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.4514998529555926},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-1106","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"1106","title":"Scientific Integrity Act","introducedDate":"2025-02-06","latestActionDate":"2025-02-06","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on Science, Space, and Technology.","poli